[{"id":4221994585,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOAO6SJc77poZZ","user":{"login":"mihe","id":4884246,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ4ODQyNDY=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4884246?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe","html_url":"https://github.com/mihe","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"I don't have final say over whether this should be exposed or not, but since I was involved in pushing the proposal for this, I do think this would be great to expose. I'm sure it would be useful to addon authors (or internal tooling in our client's case) who wish to use the same fuzzy searching as Godot.\n\nSo in hopes of getting the ball rolling here I've done a first review pass on this, and found a couple of things that might need resolving first.\n\nOutside of the review comments, one other (more general) nitpick is that none of the `private` C++ methods are underscored with prefix, as is usually the convention in Godot.\n\n---\n\nAnswering the questions/remarks you've brought up:\n\n> Is there a better way to bind all the properties to the new classes? It feels fairly verbose as it is.\n\nThe way you've done it is what I'm used to seeing. One nitpick would be to group all the `ADD_PROPERTY` stuff together as the last thing in `_bind_methods`, which is how the rest of the codebase usually organizes it. So not grouped together with their setter/getter, like what you've done.\n\n> Wrapping the results in refcounted is ~8% slower than not doing so. Is it worth still having both sets of methods so engine uses can be that tiny bit faster?\n\nYou might have looked at this already, but the way `RegEx` is implemented/exposed/used in the engine (seen [here](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/16bb065ac31b79c0ed24b3b944d3b5275123e854/modules/regex/regex.h)) is a pretty good reference to use for the design of this class. Following that example it would suggest only having the `RefCounted` API is the way to go. I would lean towards that as well.\n\n> Should the new classes be resources or is refcounted fine? It seems sort of valid for the search class to be a resource so people can save a particular configuration without needing to set it up all in code.\n\nI would not make any of this a `Resource`, again using `RegEx` as an example, where it's just `RefCounted`.\n\n> With that in mind, to me it doesn't seem worth having both sets of methods around but I do think working with the `Vector<FuzzySearchResult>` is marginally nicer since there are no variant conversions.\n\nThe way you're doing it now, with having a special bound method that returns `TypedArray`, is how I'm used to seeing it. You obviously need `TypedArray` for the script bindings and it should ideally not be forced upon the internal C++ usage, in my opinion.\n\nFunnily enough this sort of goes against using `RegEx` as reference, since it does in fact use `TypedArray<RegExMatch>` in its `search_all`, but I would probably argue that should not be the case.","state":"CHANGES_REQUESTED","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/107126#pullrequestreview-4221994585","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/107126","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/107126#pullrequestreview-4221994585"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.c